WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:03.240
- [Narrator 1] With 500
million monthly users,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.720
Spotify is the world's largest
music streaming service.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.480
- [Narrator 2] Spotify
is the home of audio.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.580
- [Narrator 1] It's known for
its personalized playlists,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.920
made with its recommendation algorithm.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.800
- Think about users as this raw material,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.870
and then on top of the data layer,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.330
we're able to build shared models.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.410
- [Narrator 1] But relying so much

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.110
on artificial intelligence
has also drawn criticism

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.860
from some industry experts
worried about algorithmic bias.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.720
Here's how Spotify uses AI

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.140
to personalize users
experiences on the platform.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.723
This is the tech behind Spotify.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.410
In the early two thousands,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.260
many people found music
recommendations through top charts

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.260
and early streaming platforms
like Pandora and Last.fm.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.720
- [Narrator 3] With the
Last.fm app from the App Store,

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.400
you can listen to great bands.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.160
- [Narrator 1] So, when Spotify
entered the scene in 2008.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.020
- It's not so much that
they the first people

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.080
to start using analytics
to recommend music,

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:58.913
but it was the way

00:00:58.913 --> 00:01:02.640
in which they combined various
computational techniques

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.780
in order to make their
recommendations feel more lifelike.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.730
- [Narrator 1] Thomas
Hodgson studies algorithms

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.080
and artificial intelligence

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.060
with a focus on how new technology

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.640
from music streaming
companies impact artists.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.550
- Fans who have listened
to Discover Weekly

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.170
and Daily Mix.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.910
The way that they talk about them

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.560
is in very human-like terms.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.260
- [Narrator 4] Discover
Weekly, you magnificent.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.060
You've done it again.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.200
- [Narrator 1] In 2014,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.380
Spotify acquired music
analytics firm The Echo Nest,

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:32.730
which blended machine learning

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.200
and natural language processing

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.780
to build a database of songs and artists.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.110
Spotify says this technology
marked an important step

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.453
in the evolution of its
recommendation system.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.060
So, how does that system work?

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.790
It starts with a process
called collaborative filtering.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.130
- Collaborative filtering
looks at the pattern,

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.400
across all of this data
and tries to understand

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.570
when do tracks happen to be
playlisted together very often?

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:02.280
You can think of it as building
a map of music and podcast.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.470
- [Narrator 1] That map
looks something like this.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.505
Each point represents a different track

00:02:06.505 --> 00:02:08.040
in Spotify's catalog

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.390
and the location of each point

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.430
is determined by collaborative filtering.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.340
- Which means that these
tracks go together,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.770
according to the way
users have playlisted them

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:17.820
and listened to them.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:18.990
- [Narrator 1] So, if these two songs,

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.700
are frequently playlisted together

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.710
they will be close to
each other in this map.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.230
Whereas if these songs are
never playlisted together

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.360
they will be farther apart in the map.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.070
But recommendations based purely

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.380
on collaborative filtering aren't perfect.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.060
For example, during the holidays,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.490
Mariah Carey's "All I want
for Christmas is You,"

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.250
might get playlisted more
frequently with "Silent Night,"

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.299
Even though this sounds like a pop song.

00:02:40.299 --> 00:02:44.850
♪ Baby all I want for Christmas is you ♪

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.916
And this sounds like a Christmas Carol.

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:49.499
♪ Silent night ♪

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.070
If Spotify only generated recommendations,

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.210
based on proximity,

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.890
then users who like Mariah Carey,

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.630
might get recommended "Silent Night"

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.570
when they aren't interested
in Christmas Carols.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:01.500
To prevent this,

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.900
Spotify adds another layer of analysis,

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.120
called content-based filtering.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.940
This algorithm gathers
metadata like the release date

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.940
and label and executes
a raw audio analysis.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.700
It uses metrics like
dance ability and loudness

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.580
to describe the sonic
characteristics of the track.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.530
These are the results for "Uptown Funk"

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.111
which sounds like this.

00:03:21.111 --> 00:03:22.829
♪ This hit, that ice cold ♪

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:25.025
♪ Michelle Pfeiffer, that white gold ♪

00:03:25.025 --> 00:03:27.810
And has the Dance Ability score of 0.856

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.940
on a scale of zero to one.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.690
The algorithm also dissects
each track's temporal structure.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.130
Here's the visual representation

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.620
of that for "Anti-Hero" by Taylor Swift.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.803
These are the beats, the
bars and the sections.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.620
Content-based filtering also takes

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.050
into account attracts cultural context

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:47.700
which means studying the lyrics

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.500
and analyzing the adjectives used

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.140
to describe the track
in articles and blogs.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.780
These filtering techniques
are not unique to Spotify,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.810
but industry experts say
what sets the platform apart

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.610
is the amount of user data it has

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.800
and the products it creates from it.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.590
Spotify says its content
based filtering technology

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:05.970
has evolved over the years

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.477
and now includes more advanced
proprietary facing features.

00:04:09.477 --> 00:04:10.310
(upbeat music)

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.360
But Hodgson says the
danger with algorithms

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.030
is that they could
reinforce existing biases.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.850
- This could mean that a
particular catalog of music

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.270
has more male artists than female artists.

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:23.490
One of the dangers with machine learning

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:27.210
is that as listeners start to engage

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:31.050
with that catalog those
biases become magnified

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.200
and that this creates what's
called a kind of feedback loop.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.690
- [Narrator 1] Spotify says
it's research teams evaluate

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.330
and mitigate against potential
algorithmic inequities

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.960
and harms and strive for
transparency about its impact.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.840
Another criticism is that
the algorithm isn't optimized

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.240
for new artists because
there's no user data.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.490
This is known as the cold start problem.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.860
Sultan says this is where human editors,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.680
play a significant role in
delivering recommendations.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.930
- They're possibly some of
the best people in the world

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.970
at trying to understand new releases

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.590
and culture and what's relevant.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:02.520
- [Narrator 1] But Hodgson says,

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.980
the bigger concern is
that certain metrics used

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.340
in the platform's audio analysis
might be culturally biased.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.720
- In other parts of the world,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.810
they have musical systems
and musical cultures

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.550
that are entirely different.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.994
- Like this North Indian
classical track for example.

00:05:16.994 --> 00:05:20.040
(gentle music)

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.790
Spotify's algorithm labels
its key signature as E minor

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.770
which Hodgson says is inappropriate

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.240
for this musical tradition.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.510
- However, it's still
the case that the music

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:33.510
that is emerging from South Asia

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.340
is being understood algorithmically,

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.760
under the Western equal temperament scale.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.740
- [Narrator 1] Spotify
says the audio analysis

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.900
is one small part of the overall system,

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.760
which takes into account many factors,

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.890
before making a recommendation.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.110
Some industry experts also point to issues

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:51.090
with how the system understands metadata

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:52.500
for classical music.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.170
For example, the metadata
for a Tchaikovsky track,

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.870
can include not just the name
of the work and the artist,

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:00.600
but also the movement,
opus number and conductor.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.900
Spotify's algorithm
isn't optimized for that.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.270
Apple Music, which has
emerged in recent years

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.830
as a competitor to Spotify,

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:09.300
released a new app in March

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.244
that the company says is
designed to solve this problem.

00:06:12.244 --> 00:06:16.140
(gentle orchestral music)

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.970
Spotify says it doesn't comment

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.871
on a competitor's marketing campaigns.

00:06:19.871 --> 00:06:23.340
In February, the streaming
service joined the recent buzz,

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:24.660
around generative AI.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.370
- [X] I'm X, and from this moment on

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.640
I'm gonna be your own
personal AI DJ on Spotify.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.070
- [Narrator 1] The DJ gives
the algorithm a human voice

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.110
and offers listeners additional context,

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.120
around a recommendation.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:36.953
- [X] Up next,

00:06:36.953 --> 00:06:39.330
I know you've been on a
summer song kick lately.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:40.163
- [Narrator 1] Sultan says,

00:06:40.163 --> 00:06:42.900
the company is also exploring
reinforcement learning,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.120
a technique that would allow
the recommendation system

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.550
to learn automatically based on feedback.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:50.850
- It will help with the diversity
of their recommendation,

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.710
it will help with the
longer term retention

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.140
and we are trying to
push the state-of-the-art

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.330
in each of those,
introducing new technologies,

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.576
new capabilities and
bringing new experiences.

00:07:00.576 --> 00:07:03.243
(upbeat music)

